== Chapter VII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The Captivating power of Indwelling-Sin, wherein it consisteth. The prevalency of Sin, when from itself, when from Temptation. The Rage and Madness that is in Sin.]
]

#smallcaps[The] third thing assigned unto this #emph[Law of Sin] in its opposition unto God, and the Law of his Grace, is, that it #emph[leads the soul Captive,] Rom 7:23. #emph[I find a Law leading me Captive, (Captivating me) unto the Law of Sin.] And this is the utmost height which the Apostle in that place carries the opposition and warring of the remainders of Indwelling-Sin unto; closing the consideration of it with a complaint of the state and condition of Believers thereby; and an earnest prayer for deliverance from it, v. 24. #emph[O wretched man that I am, who shall deliver me from this body of death.] What is contained in this expression, and intended by it, shall be declared in the ensuing Observations.

First, It is not directly the #emph[power] and actings of the Law of Sin that are here expressed, but its #emph[success] in and upon its actings. But #emph[success] is the greatest evidence of power, and #emph[leading Captive] in War is the height of #emph[success.] None can aim at greater #emph[success,] than to lead their Enemies Captive. And it is a peculiar expression in the Scripture of #emph[great success.] So the Lord Christ on his victory over Satan, is said to #emph[lead Captivity Captive,] Eph 4:8. That is, to conquer him, who had conquered and prevailed upon others. And this he did when by death he #emph[destroyed him that had the power of death, that is the Devil,] Heb 2:14. Here then a great #emph[prevalency] and power of sin in its warring against the soul is discovered. It so wars, as to #emph[lead Captive;] which had it not great power, it could not do, especially against that #emph[resistance] of the soul which is included in this expression.

Secondly, It is said, that it leads the soul captive #emph[unto the Law of Sin.] Not to this or that sin, particular sin, actual sin, but to the #emph[Law of Sin.] God for the most part ordereth things so, and gives out such supplies of grace unto Believers, as that they shall not be made a prey unto #emph[this] or #emph[that] particular sin, that it should prevail in them, and compel them to serve it in the lusts thereof, that it should have Dominion over them, that they should be #emph[Captives] and slaves unto it. This is that which #emph[David] prays so earnestly against, Ps 19:12, 13. #emph[Cleanse thou me from secret faults, keep back thy servant also from presumptuous sins, let them not have dominion over me, then shall I be upright.] He supposeth the continuance of the Law of Sin in him, v. 12, which will bring forth #emph[errors of life,] and secret sins against which he findeth relief in pardoning and cleansing mercy which he prays for. This saith he will be my condition. But for #emph[sins of pride] and boldness, such as all sins are, that get #emph[Dominion] in a man, that make a #emph[Captive] of a man, the Lord restrain thy servant from them. For what sin soever gets such power in a man, be it in its own nature small or great, it becomes in him in whom it is, a sin of #emph[boldness, pride,] and presumption. For these things are not reckoned from the nature or kind of the sin, but from its prevalency and customariness, wherein its pride, boldness, and contempt of God doth consist. To the same purpose, if I mistake not, prays #emph[Jabez,] 1 Chr 4:10. #emph[O that thou wouldst bless me indeed and enlarge my Coast, and that thine hand may be with me, and that thou wouldst keep me from evil, that it may not grieve me.] The holy man took occasion from his #emph[own name] to pray against sin that that might not be a #emph[grief and sorrow] to him by its power and prevalency. I confess sometimes it may come to this with a Believer, that for a season he may be #emph[led Captive] by some particular sin. It may have so much prevalency #emph[in] him, as to have power #emph[over] him. So it seems to have been with #emph[David] when he lay so long in his sin without repentance. And was plainly so with those in Isaiah 57:17, 18. #emph[For the iniquity of his covetousness was I wroth, and smote him, I hid me, and was wroth, and he went on frowardly in the way of his heart; I have seen his ways, and will heal him.] They continued under the power of their #emph[covetousness:] so that no dealings of God with them, for so long a time could reclaim them. But for the most part when any Lust or Sin doth so prevail, it is from the advantage and furtherance that it hath got by some #emph[powerful temptation] of Satan. He hath poisoned it, inflamed it, and entangled the soul. So the Apostle speaking of such as through sin were fallen off from their holiness says, #emph[they were in the snare of the Devil, being taken Captive by him at his will,] 2 Tim 2:26. Though it were their own lust, that they served, yet they were brought into bondage thereunto, by being entangled in some snare of Satan. And thence they are said to be #emph[taken alive,] as a poor Beast in a toil.

And here, by the way we may a little inquire whether the prevailing power of a particular sin in any, be from itself, or from the influence of #emph[temptation] upon it, concerning which at present take only these two Observations.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, Much of the #emph[prevalency] of sin upon the soul is certainly from #emph[Satan,] when the perplexing and captivating sin hath no peculiar footing, nor advantage in the nature, constitution, or condition of the sinner. When any #emph[lust] grows high and prevailing more than others upon its own account, it is from the peculiar advantage that it hath in the natural constitution, or the station or condition of the person in the world. For otherwise the Law of Sin gives an equal propensity unto all evil, an equal vigor unto every lust. When therefore it cannot be discerned, that the #emph[captivating] sin is peculiarly fixed in the nature of the sinner, or is advantaged from his education or employment in the world, the prevalency of it is peculiarly from Satan. He hath got to the root of it, and hath given it poison and strength. Yea, perhaps sometimes that which may seem to the #emph[soul] to be the corrupt #emph[lusting] of the heart, is nothing but Satan's imposing his suggestions on the imagination. If then a man find an importunate rage from any corruption, that is not evidently seated in his nature: Let him as the Papists say, #emph[cross] himself, or fly by faith to the Cross of Christ, for the Devil is nigh at hand.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, When a Lust is prevalent unto #emph[Captivity] where it brings in no advantage to the #emph[flesh,] it is from Satan. All that the Law of Sin doth of itself, is to serve the #emph[providence of the flesh,] Rom 13:14. And it must bring in unto it somewhat of the profits and pleasures that are its object. Now if the prevailing sin do not so act itself, if it be more spiritual and inward, it is much from Satan by the imagination, more than the corruption of the heart itself. But this by the way.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I say then, that the Apostle treats not here, of our being #emph[captivated] unto this or that sin, but unto the #emph[Law of Sin.] That is, we are compelled to bear its presence and burden whether we will or no. Sometimes the soul thinks or hopes that it may through grace be #emph[utterly freed] from this troublesome Inmate. Upon some sweet enjoyment of God, some full supply of grace, some return from wandering, some deep affliction, some thorough humiliation, the poor soul begins to hope that it shall now be freed from the Law of Sin. But after a while it perceives that it is quite otherwise. Sin acts again, makes good its old station, and the soul finds that whether it will or no, it must bear its yoke. This makes it sigh and cry out for deliverance.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, This leading Captive argues a prevalency against the #emph[renitency] or contrary actings of the will. This is intimated plainly in this expression; namely, that the will opposeth, and makes head as it were against the working of sin. This the Apostle declares in those expressions which he uses, vv. 15, 19, 20. And herein consists the #emph[lusting of the Spirit] against the flesh, Gal 5:17. That is, the contending of grace to expel and subdue it. The spiritual habits of grace that are in the #emph[Will,] do so resist and act against it. And the excitation of those habits by the Spirit are directed to the same purpose. This leading #emph[Captive,] is contrary I say, to the inclinations and actings of the #emph[renewed will.] No man is made a Captive but against his will. Captivity is misery and trouble, and no man willingly puts himself into trouble. Men choose it in its #emph[causes,] and in the ways and means leading unto it, but not in itself. So the Prophet informs us, Hos 5:11. #emph[Ephraim was not willingly oppressed and broken in Judgment;] that was his misery and trouble; but he willingly walked after the Commandment of the #emph[Idolatrous Kings] which brought him thereunto. Whatever consent then the soul may give unto sin, which is the means of this Captivity, it gives none to the #emph[Captivity] itself; that is against the will wholly. Hence these things ensue.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, #emph[That the power of sin is great,] which is that which we are in the demonstration of; and this appears in its prevalency unto Captivity; against the actings and contendings of the will for liberty from it. Had it no opposition made unto it, or were its Adversary, weak, negligent, slothful, it were no great evidence of its power, that it made Captives. But its prevailing against diligence, activity, watchfulness, the constant renitency of the will, this evinceth its efficacy.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, This leading Captive intimates #emph[manifold particular successes.] Had it not success in particular it could not be said at all to lead captive. Rebel it might, assail it might, but it cannot be said to #emph[lead Captive] without some successes. And there are several degrees of the success of the Law of Sin in the soul. Sometimes it carries the Person unto outward #emph[actual sin] which is its utmost aim. Sometimes it obtaineth the #emph[consent of the will,] but is cast out by grace, and proceeds no farther. Sometimes it wearies and entangles the soul, that it turns aside as it were, and leaves contending which is a success also. One or more, or all of these must be, where #emph[Captivity] takes place. Such a kind of course doth the Apostle ascribe unto covetousness, 1 Tim 6:9.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, This leading captive manifests this condition #emph[to be miserable and wretched.] To be thus yoked and dealt withal against the judgment of the mind, the choice and consent of the will, its utmost strivings and contendings, how sad is it? When the neck is sore and tender with former pressures, to be compelled to bear the yoke again, this pierces, this grieves, this even breaks the heart. When the soul is principled by grace unto a #emph[loathing] of sin, of every evil way, to an hatred of the least discrepancy between itself and the holy will of God, then to be imposed on by this #emph[Law of sin,] with all that enmity and folly, that deadness and filth, wherewith it is attended: what more dreadful condition? All captivity is dreadful in its own nature; the greatest aggravation of it is from the condition of the Tyrant unto whom any one is captivated. Now what can be worse than this Law of sin? Hence the Apostle having once mentioned this captivity, cries out as one quite weary and ready to faint, v. 24.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, This condition is #emph[peculiar to Believers.] Unregenerate men are not said to be #emph[led captive] to the Law of sin. They may indeed be led captive unto this or that particular sin or corruption, that is, they may be forced to serve it against the power of their convictions. They are convinced of the evil of it, an adulterer of his uncleanness, a drunkard of his abomination, and make some resolutions, it may be, against it. But their lust is too hard for them, they cannot cease to sin, and so are made captives or slaves to this or that particular sin. But they cannot be said to be #emph[led captive to the Law of sin,] and that because they are willingly subject thereunto. It hath, as it were, a rightful dominion over them, and they oppose it not, but only when it hath irruptions to the disturbance of their consciences. And then the opposition they make unto it is not from their #emph[wills,] but is the mere acting of an affrighted conscience, and a convinced mind. They regard not the #emph[nature] of sin, but its #emph[guilt] and consequences. But to be brought into captivity, is that which befalls a man against his will. Which is all that shall be spoken unto this degree of the actings of the power of sin, manifesting itself in its success.]]

=== The Rage and Madness of Sin

The fourth and last degree of the opposition made by the Law of sin to God, and the Law of his Will and Grace, is in its #emph[Rage] and #emph[Madness.] There is #emph[Madness] in its nature, Eccl 9:3. #emph[The heart of the sons of men is full of evil, and madness is in their heart.] The evil that the heart of man is full of by nature, is that #emph[Indwelling-sin] whereof we speak. And this is so in their heart, that it riseth up unto #emph[madness.] The Holy Ghost expresseth this rage of sin by a fit similitude which he useth in sundry places, as Jer 2:24. Hos 8:9. It maketh #emph[men as a wild Ass;] she traverseth her ways, and snuffeth up the wind, and runneth whither her mind or lust leads her. And he saith of Idolaters enraged with their lusts, that they #emph[are mad upon their idols,] Jer 50:38. We may a little consider what lies in this #emph[madness] and rage of sin, and how it riseth up thereunto.

First, For the #emph[nature] of it, it seems to consist in a violent, heady, pertinacious pressing unto evil or sin. #emph[Violence,] importunity and pertinacy are in it. It is the tearing and torturing of the soul by any sin to force its consent, and to obtain satisfaction. It riseth up in the heart, is denied by the Law of grace, and rebuked; it returns and exerts its poison again; the soul is startled, casts it off; it returns again with new violence and importunity, the soul cries out for help and deliverance, looks round about to all springs of Gospel grace and relief, trembles at the furious assaults of sin, and casts itself into the arms of Christ for deliverance. And if it be not able to take that course, it is foiled and hurried up and down through the mire and filth of foolish imaginations, corrupt and noisome lusts, which rend and tear it, as if they would devour its whole spiritual life and power. See 1 Tim 6:9, 10. 2 Pet 2:14. It was not much otherwise with them whom we instanced in before, Isa 57:17. They had an inflamed enraged lust working in them; even #emph[covetousness,] or the love of this world, by which, as the Apostle speaks, men #emph[pierce themselves through with many sorrows.] God is angry with them, and discovereth his wrath by all the ways and means that it was possible for them to be made sensible thereof. He was #emph[wroth] and smote them; but though it may be this staggered them a little, yet they went on. #emph[He is angry, and hides himself from them,] deserts them as to his gracious assisting comforting Presence. Doth this work the effect? No they go on #emph[frowardly] still, as men mad on their covetousness. Nothing can put a stop to their raging lusts. This is #emph[plain madness] and fury. We need not seek far for instances, we see men mad on their lusts every day: And which is the worst kind of #emph[madness,] their lusts do not rage so much in them, as they rage in the pursuit of them. Are those greedy pursuits of things in the world, which we see some men engaged in, though they have other pretenses, indeed any thing else but plain #emph[madness] in the pursuit of their lusts? God who searcheth the hearts of men, knows, that the most of things that are done with other pretenses in the world, are nothing but the actings of men mad and furious in the pursuit of their lusts.

Secondly, That sin ariseth not unto this height ordinarily, but when it hath got a double advantage.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, That it be provoked, enraged and heightened by some great #emph[temptation.] Though it be a poison in itself, yet being inbred in nature, it grows not violently outrageous without the contribution of some new poison of #emph[Satan] unto it in a suitable temptation. It was the advantage that Satan got against #emph[David,] by a suitable temptation that raised his lusts to that #emph[rage] and #emph[madness,] which it went forth unto in the business of #emph[Bathsheba] and #emph[Uriah.] Though sin be always a fire in the bones, yet it flames not, unless Satan come with his bellows to blow it up. And let any one in whom the Law of sin ariseth to this height of rage, seriously consider, and he may find out where the devil stands and puts in the business.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, It must be advantaged by some #emph[former] entertainment and prevalency. Sin grows not to this height at its first assault. Had it not been suffered to make its entrance, had there not been some yielding in the soul, this had not come about. The great wisdom and security of the soul in dealing with in-dwelling sin, is to put a violent stop unto its #emph[beginnings,] its first motions and actings. Venture all on the first attempt. Die rather than yield one step unto it. If through the #emph[deceit] of sin, or the #emph[negligence] of the soul, or its carnal confidence, to give bounds to lusts actings at other seasons, it makes any entrance into the soul, and finds any entertainment, it gets strength and power, and insensibly ariseth to the frame under consideration. Thou hadst never had the experience of the #emph[fury] of sin, if thou hadst not been content with some of its #emph[dalliances.] Hadst thou not brought up this servant, this slave delicately, it would not have now presumed beyond a son. Now when the #emph[Law of sin] in any particular hath got this double advantage, the furtherance of a vigorous temptation, and some prevalency formerly obtained, whereby it is let into the strengths of the soul, it often riseth up to this frame whereof we speak.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, We may see what #emph[accompanies] this rage and madness, what are the properties of it, and what effects it produceth.]

==== Casting Off the Rule of Grace

First, There is in it the casting off, for a time at least, of the yoke, rule and government of the Spirit and Law of Grace. Where Grace hath the #emph[dominion,] it will never utterly be expelled from its Throne, it will still keep its right and sovereignty. But its #emph[influences] may for a season be intercepted, and its government be suspended by the power of sin. Can we think that the #emph[Law of grace] had any actual influence of rule on the heart of #emph[David,] when upon the provocation received from #emph[Nabal,] he was so hurried with the desire of self-revenge, that he cried, #emph[gird on your swords,] to his companions, and resolved not to leave alive one man of his whole household, 1 Sam 25:34. Or that #emph[Asa] was in any better frame, when he #emph[smote the Prophet,] and put him in Prison, that spake unto him in the name of the Lord. Sin in this case is like an #emph[untamed Horse,] which having first cast off his Rider, runs away with fierceness and rage. It first casts off a present sense of the yoke of Christ, and the Law of his Grace, and then hurries the soul at its pleasure. Let us a little consider how this is done.

The seat and residence of grace is in the whole soul; it is in the Inner man, it is in the mind, the will, and the affections; for the #emph[whole soul is renewed by it unto the Image of God,] Eph 4:23, 24. And the whole man is a #emph[new Creature,] 2 Cor 5:17. And in all these doth it exert its power and efficacy; Its Rule or Dominion is the pursuit of its effectual working in all the faculties of the soul, as they are one united principle of moral and spiritual operations. So then, the interrupting of its exercise, of its Rule and Power by the Law of Sin, must consist in its contrary acting in and upon the faculties and affections of the soul, whereon, and by which grace should exert its power and efficacy. And this it doth. It #emph[darkens] the mind, partly through innumerable vain prejudices, and false reasonings, as we shall see when we come to consider its #emph[deceitfulness;] and partly through the #emph[steaming] of the Affections, heated with the noisome lusts that have laid hold on them. Hence that saving light that is in the mind is clouded and stifled, that it cannot put forth its #emph[transforming power] to change the soul into the likeness of Christ discovered unto it, which is its proper work, Rom 12:2. The habitual inclination of the will to obedience, which is the next way of the working of the Law of grace, is first #emph[weakened,] then cast aside, and rendered useless by the continual solicitations of sin and temptation; so that the will first lets go its hold, and disputes whether it shall yield or no; and at last gives up itself to its Adversary; and for the #emph[Affections] commonly the beginning of this evil is in them. They cross one another, and torture the soul with their impetuous violence. By this way is the Rule of the Law of Grace intercepted by the Law of Sin, even by imposing upon it in the whole #emph[seat] of its Government. When this is done, it is sad work that sin will make in the soul. The Apostle warns Believers to take heed hereof, Rom 6:12. #emph[Let not sin therefore reign in your mortal bodies that you should obey it in the lusts thereof.] Look to it that it get not the #emph[Dominion,] that it usurp not Rule, no not for a moment. It will labor to intrude itself into the #emph[Throne,] watch against it, or a woeful state and condition lies at the door. This then accompanies this rage and madness of the Law of Sin. It casts off during its prevalency the Rule of the Law of Grace wholly. It speaks in the soul, but is not heard, it commands the contrary, but is not obeyed. It cries out, #emph[do not this abominable thing which the Lord hateth,] but is not regarded; that is, not so far as to be able to put a present stop to the rage of sin, and to recover its own rule, which God in his own time restores to it by the power of his Spirit dwelling in us.

==== Fearlessness and Contempt of Danger

Secondly, #emph[Madness] or rage are accompanied with #emph[fearlessness] and contempt of danger. It takes away the power of consideration, and all that influence that it ought to have upon the soul. Hence sinners that are wholly under the power of this rage, are said, #emph[to run upon God and the thick bosses of his buckler,] Job 16:21. That wherein he is armed for their utter ruin. They despise the utmost that he can do to them, being secretly resolved to accomplish their lusts though it cost them their souls. Some few considerations will farther clear this unto us.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, Ofttimes when the soul is broken loose from the power of #emph[renewing grace,] God deals with it to keep it within bounds by #emph[preventing grace.] So the Lord declares that he will deal with #emph[Israel,] Hos 2:6. Seeing thou hast rejected me, I will take another course with thee. I will lay obstacles before thee that thou shalt not be able to pass on whither the fury of thy lusts would drive thee. He will propose that to them from without, that shall obstruct them in their progress.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, These #emph[hinderances] that God lays in the way of sinners, as shall be afterwards at large declared; are of two sorts.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, #emph[Rational considerations] taken from the consequence of the sin and evil that the soul is solicited unto, and perplexed withal. Such are the #emph[fear of Death,] Judgment, and Hell, falling into the hands of the Living God, who is a consuming fire. Whilst a man is under the power of the Law of the Spirit of Life, the #emph[Love of Christ constraineth him,] 2 Cor 5:14. The principle of his doing good and abstaining from evil, is #emph[faith working by love,] accompanied with a following of Christ because of the sweet savor of his name. But now when this blessed #emph[easy yoke] is for a season cast off, so as was manifested before, God sets an hedge of #emph[terror] before the soul, minds it of Death and Judgment to come, flashes the flames of Hell Fire in the face, fills the soul with consideration of all the evil consequence of sin to deter it from its purpose. To this end doth he make use of all threatenings recorded in the Law and Gospel. To this Head also may be referred all the considerations that may be taken from things temporal, as shame, reproach, scandal, punishments, and the like. By the consideration of these things, I say, doth God set an #emph[hedge] before them.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, Providential Dispensations are used by the Lord to the same purpose, and these are of two sorts;]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, Such as are suited to work upon the soul, and to cause it to desist and give over in its lustings and pursuit of sin. Such are #emph[Afflictions] and Mercies, Isa 57:17. #emph[I was wroth and I smote them,] I testified my dislike of their ways by Afflictions; So Hos 2:9, 11, 12. #emph[God chastens men with pains on their bodies,] saith he, in #emph[Job, to turn them from their purpose, and to hide sin from them,] Job 33:17, 19. And other ways he hath to come to them and touch them, as in their Names, Relations, Estates and desirable things. Or else he heaps mercies on them that they may consider whom they are rebelling against. It may be signal distinguishing mercies are made their portion for many days.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, Such as #emph[actually hinder] the soul from pursuing sin, though it be resolved so to do. The various ways whereby God doth #emph[this,] we must afterwards consider.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[These are the ways I say, whereby the soul is dealt withal, after the Law of #emph[Indwelling-Sin] hath cast off for a season the influencing power of the Law of Grace. But now when #emph[lust] rises up to #emph[rage] or #emph[madness,] it will also contemn all these, even the Rod and him that hath appointed it. It will rush on shame, reproaches, wrath, and whatever may befall it. That is, though they be presented unto it, it will venture upon them all. Rage and madness is #emph[fearless.] And this it doth two ways.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, It #emph[possesseth the mind,] that it suffers not the consideration of these things to dwell upon it, but renders the thoughts of them slight and #emph[evanid.] Or if the mind do force itself to a contemplation of them, yet it interposeth between it and the #emph[Affections,] that they shall not be influenced by it in any proportion to what is required. The soul in such a condition will be able to take such things into contemplation, and not at all to be moved by them. And where they do prevail for a season, yet they are insensibly wrought off from the heart again.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, By secret stubborn #emph[resolves] to venture all upon the way wherein it is.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And this is the second branch of this Evidence of the #emph[power of sin,] taken from the opposition that it makes to the #emph[Law of Grace, as it were] by the way of force, strength and violence; the consideration of its #emph[deceit] doth now follow.]]
